        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(acp): use Object.hasOwn instead of in operator in printSessionUpdate",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe `printSessionUpdate` helper in `src/acp/client.ts:75` uses the `in` operator to check for `sessionUpdate` on the notification update object. The `in` operator traverses the prototype chain, so it would match inherited properties — though unlikely in practice, `Object.hasOwn()` is the more correct semantic for own-property checks.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nReplaced `!(sessionUpdate in update)` with `!Object.hasOwn(update, sessionUpdate)` (1 line change). `Object.hasOwn()` (ES2022) is the standard own-property check, available since Node.js 16.9+.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Vitest regression tests (3 scenarios, 54/54 pass)\n\nThe branch includes 3 regression tests in `src/acp/client.test.ts` that exercise the actual production code path against real `@agentclientprotocol/sdk` SessionNotification discriminator types:\n\n1. **Valid session updates** — `Object.hasOwn` correctly identifies own-property on ACP SessionNotification updates\n2. **Non-sessionUpdate notifications** — correctly rejected\n3. **Prototype-injected sessionUpdate** — rejected by `Object.hasOwn` but matched by `in` operator (demonstrates the fix prevents prototype pollution)\n\n### CI\n\nAll 70 CI checks pass on the latest commit.\n\n### Maintainer proof override requested\n\nPer ClawSweeper's guidance: this is a defensive cleanup (not a user-visible bug), so real ACP client terminal output is impractical. A narrow maintainer proof override is the recommended path.\n\nLikely owner: @steipete — introduced the interactive ACP client harness.\n\n## Issue\n\nFixes #90290\n\n---\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
